    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Staff-Piano-Teacher_R003282": {
      "title": "Staff Piano Teacher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-13\nPart time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Staff Piano Teacher Job Family: Temp Full-Time/Part-Time: Part time Compensation Grade: TMP Pay Type: Hourly Department: JM Music Job Summary: Piano for Young People is a pre-college piano program for students kindergarten through high school. Students pursue piano study through private lessons in a university setting. Materials covered in lessons include sight reading, music theory, music history, ear training, and an introduction to a wide range of musical genres and styles of the teacher and student’s choosing. Staff teachers in Piano for Young People are responsible for teaching weekly lessons on campus, planning and preparing for individualized lessons, sending weekly assignments and reminders to students and parents, meeting with the director as needed, and regular correspondence. Staff teachers also attend recitals and competition events throughout the academic year. Staff teachers are committed to professional development and growth in knowledge of pedagogical practices. Job Description: Teaching Teaches weekly individualized piano lessons to students ages five and older, including sight reading, music theory, music history, ear training, technique, and repertoire. Creates and fosters relationships with students and families. Teaches in person and virtually as required by the program and university. Planning Prepares weekly for lessons through communication with students and parents, writing weekly assignments, and coordinating with the director. Pursues professional development to stay knowledgeable of pedagogical practices. Performs other duties as assigned by the director. Recitals & Events Prepares students for and attends recitals and other performances throughout the academic year. Communication Communicates regularly with students/parents, director, and others. Reports To Director of Piano for Young People Requirements/Qualifications Bachelor’s degree or higher in Music, Piano, or Piano Pedagogy. Prior teaching experience Preferred Knowledge, Abilities, and Skills Knowledge of pedagogical practices, piano method books, and sequencing instruction Adept at working with children Adaptable and open to trying new pedagogical methods Proficient and comfortable with virtual lessons and digital communication Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Part-Time-Counselor_R003399": {
      "title": "Part-Time Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nPart time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Part-Time Counselor Job Family: Professionals Full-Time/Part-Time: Part time Pay Type: Hourly Department: Counseling Center Job Summary: The Mental Health Counselor is responsible for providing culturally competent mental health services to university students. This role involves individual and group counseling, crisis intervention, outreach, and collaboration with university departments to foster an inclusive and supportive environment for students. The counselor will work part-time, contributing to the mental health and well-being of students while promoting a campus culture of inclusivity and acceptance. Job Description: Key Responsibilities: 1. Individual Counseling:    - Provide one-on-one counseling sessions to students, addressing a variety of mental health issues such as anxiety, depression, identity issues, trauma, and relationship concerns.    - Develop and implement personalized treatment plans and therapeutic interventions. 2. Group Counseling:    - Facilitate support groups and therapy groups for students    - Create a safe and supportive group environment that encourages open discussion and mutual support. 3. Crisis Intervention:    - Offer crisis intervention services, including risk assessment and management of immediate psychological crises.    - Collaborate with university crisis response teams to ensure timely and effective support for students in distress. 4. Outreach and Education:    - Develop and deliver educational workshops, seminars, and training sessions on mental health topics for students, faculty, and staff.    - Participate in campus-wide events and initiatives to promote mental health awareness and inclusivity. 5. Consultation and Collaboration:    - Consult with faculty, staff, and administration on issues related to student mental health and well-being.    - Collaborate with university departments to enhance support services and programming. 6. Documentation:    - Maintain accurate and confidential client records in compliance with legal and ethical standards.     7. Professional Development:    - Stay current with best practices in university mental health counseling through ongoing professional development and training.    - Participate in regular supervision and case consultation meetings. Qualifications: - Master’s degree in counseling, Psychology, Social Work, or a related field from an accredited institution. - Licensed or licensed eligible in SC - Minimum of 2 years experience in mental health counseling. - Demonstrated knowledge of young adult mental health issues and cultural competency. - Strong crisis intervention skills and experience. - Excellent communication, interpersonal, and organizational skills. - Ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Preferred Qualifications: - Experience working with young adults. - Experience working with college athletes. - Bilingual or multilingual skills. - Training in trauma-informed care and working with ADHD and Autism Hours and Compensation: - Part-time position - Flexible Hours and Days - Available during business hours on Mondays, Thursdays and Fridays Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Costume-Shop-Supervisor_R003396": {
      "title": "Costume Shop Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Costume Shop Supervisor Job Family: Professionals Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Salary Department: Theatre Arts Job Summary: This is a professional staff position reporting to the Department Chair of Theatre Arts. This person is responsible for overseeing all day-to-day operations of the Theatre Arts Department Costume Shop, costume storage facilities, wardrobe operations, and costume production support for the Theatre Arts Department classes and production seasons, including primarily the Furman Theatre Main Stage productions and secondarily the Furman Music Department Sarah Reese Lyric Theatre productions (a minimum of 5 productions per academic year). Work includes the selection, assignment, training, and evaluation of student shop assistants and wardrobe crews; ordering supplies; maintaining tools, machinery, and costume stock inventories; reconciling production and lab budgets; patternmaking/draping; cutting and stitching; organizing and directing fittings; and supervising made-to-order, altered, shopped, and pulled costumes. This person will have the ability to instruct and supervise students in costume shop laboratory settings and assist with courses related to costume technology, crafts, and design. This position contributes directly to the academic advising environment, Undergraduate Research Fellowships, Theatre Practicum, and high-impact student mentorship in collaboration with department faculty and The Furman Advantage. Job Description: Responsibilities: 1.    Schedules, trains, and oversees student costume shop assistants, wardrobe crew heads, and run crews to meet departmental standards, safety protocols, and production deadlines.  2.    Establishes and maintains effective working relationships and provides guidance and excellent service to faculty, students, staff, guest designers, visiting artists, and production personnel.  3.    Works closely with the Department Chair, Technical Director, and each production director in establishing and maintaining the costume, fitting, and wardrobe calendars in coordination with the department production schedule.  4.    Consults and collaborates with faculty designers, guest designers, student designers, and directors to interpret design renderings, plates, and research into finished garments and accessories.  5.    Serves in multiple supervisory and technical roles, including Costume Shop Supervisor, Draper/Cutter, Wardrobe Supervisor, Costume Crafts Technician, and Costume Stock/Storage Manager.  6.    Plans, organizes, and directs the realization of costumes for all Furman Theatre Main Stage and Sarah Reese Lyric Theatre productions, including patterning, flat patterning, draping, cutting, stitching, alteration, dyeing, distressing, millinery, and crafts.  7.    Organizes, schedules, and directs costume fittings, ensuring timely completion of garments, necessary alterations, and seamless transition from the build phase through dress rehearsals and performance runs.  8.    Hires, mentors, and trains student workers and practicum students in costume construction techniques, sewing machine and specialty equipment operation, shop safety, garment care, and wardrobe protocols.  9.    Mentors student designers and crew heads as part of the Undergraduate Research Fellowship program, Theatre Practicum, and independent student projects in costume design, costume technology, crafts, and wardrobe management.  10.    Assists in the teaching of costume crafts and technology courses, and directly supervises laboratory hours, assignments, and practical skill development associated with those courses.  11.    Oversees wardrobe operations during technical/dress rehearsals and performance runs, including backstage dressing procedures, quick changes, hair/makeup/wig care, laundry, daily maintenance, repair, and strike.  12.    Performs administrative tasks as needed, including production reports, supply orders, invoice processing, purchasing reconciliation, budget tracking, and vendor coordination.  13.    Maintains costume budgets and resources, ensuring responsible allocation of funds for departmental productions, shop operations, and teaching labs.  14.    Manages costume inventory, stock, and rental/loan agreements, including the organization, pulling, cataloging, cleaning, restock, storage, and loaning of departmental garments, footwear, and accessories.  15.    May serve as Costume Designer for select productions in the Furman Theatre Main Stage season as assigned.  16.    Enforces and maintains knowledge of all relevant safety protocols, chemical safety (dyeing/distressing), OSHA guidelines, theatrical industry practices, and institutional Fire and Life Safety Codes.  17.    Maintains, inspects, and coordinates routine maintenance/repairs for all costume shop equipment, including domestic and industrial sewing machines, sergers, irons, steamers, cutting tables, dye vats, and ventilation systems; informs supervisor of equipment needs and manages replacements.  18.    Reports facility, space, or maintenance issues to facilities services and the Department Chair to ensure shop cleanliness, air quality, safety, and proper functionality of teaching and production spaces.  19.    Able to work in an organized manner and adjust flexibly to last-minute adjustments, design evolutions, and tight turnaround times in a fast-paced, collaborative production environment.  20.    Competent in Microsoft Office, e-mail, Box file sharing, budget systems, and digital communication tools or willingness to learn; working knowledge of digital patterning/rendering tools, costume inventory databases, or willingness to learn emerging shop technologies.  21.    Provides incident, safety, and facility reports to the Department Chair and appropriate campus safety authorities when required.  22.    Communicates effectively and with diplomacy, tact, and confidentiality when working with students, guest artists, faculty, staff, parents, and the public; actively recognizes and supports student contributions and growth.  23.    Participates in departmental activities and recruitment, contributing significantly to the Theatre Practicum component of the student experience and student engagement within The Furman Advantage.  24.    Pursues ongoing professional development in costume technology, crafts and technology trends, theatrical techniques, shop management, and higher-education safety standards.  25.    Completes annual performance review process with supervisor to align individual goals with departmental, division, and university strategic priorities.  26.    Performs other duties as assigned. The intent of this job classification description is to provide a representative summary of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions in this classification and shall not be construed as a declaration of the specific duties and responsibilities of any particular position. Employees may be requested to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically present in this description. Relationships: Continuous contact with members of the Theatre Arts Department, immediate supervisor (Department Chair), Technical Director, faculty designers/directors, music/opera faculty, guest artists, and student employees/practicum students for planning, coordinating, problem-solving, fitting execution, and mentoring toward shared artistic and educational goals. Parties generally maintain cooperative and collaborative working attitudes. Frequent contact with students, campus partners, vendors, and the general public to exchange factual information, schedule fittings, enforce facility and safety procedures, coordinate costume loans/rentals, and discuss non-controversial departmental operations. Responsibility for Final Decisions: Responsible for own work and direct oversight of shop operations, shop safety, fitting workflow, wardrobe execution, and student shop assistants. May assist, instruct, and evaluate students and crew members performing technical costume work. Reviews work to ensure compliance with production designs, timelines, safety standards, and departmental budgets. Tasks involve interrelated artistic and technical processes requiring professional judgment, creative problem-solving, and adherence to established institutional and safety guidelines. Errors may cause delays in production schedules, costume quality issues, budget variances, or safety hazards, requiring prompt corrective action within the unit. Reports to: Chair, Theatre Arts Department Work is performed independently within broad departmental guidelines, except where institutional policy, academic regulation, or budgetary exceptions require chair approval. Education/Skill Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in Theatre Arts, Costume Technology, Costume Design, Fashion Design, or a related field. 3–5 years of professional or academic experience in costume construction, shop operations, patterning/draping, cutting, stitching, fitting, and wardrobe run crew supervision. Demonstrated technical expertise in flat patterning, draping, tailoring, garment construction, costume crafts, dyeing/distressing, and alteration techniques. Knowledge of theatrical production processes, stage terminology, fitting etiquette, fabric science, and costume shop safety standards. Practical knowledge of wigs, hair, stage makeup, millinery, or specialty craft construction. Strong organizational, multitasking, and time-management skills with the ability to manage costume requirements across multiple concurrent productions (e.g., Main Stage theatre and lyric theatre/opera). Strong interpersonal and communication skills; ability to work collaboratively with diverse teams of faculty, guest directors, designers, and students. Proficiency with office software (word processing, spreadsheets, file sharing, e-mail) and basic budget tracking/reconciliation. If interested in designing for the department, demonstrated experience designing costumes for mainstage theatre, musicals, or lyric theatre/opera productions is preferred. Work conditions: Work requires: Frequent reaching, handling, fine-motor manipulation (cutting, pinning, hand/machine sewing). Frequent stooping, kneeling, crouching, and bending during fittings, alterations, and storage management. Frequent standing and walking for extended periods during shop work, fittings, and dress rehearsals. Frequent pushing, pulling, lifting, and carrying of costume racks, fabric bolts, dress forms, and storage bins (able to lift and transport up to 40–50 lbs). Exposure to costume shop equipment, irons/steamers, dyes, adhesives, aerosols, and laundry chemicals with appropriate ventilation and PPE. Schedule: Standard weekday afternoon shop hours with significant evening and weekend work required during tech weeks, dress rehearsals, fitting periods, strikes, and performance runs. Irregular production schedule based on the performance calendar. This is a 10-month position. Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Research-Associate_R003402": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Research Assistant/Associate Job Family: Professionals Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Salary Department: IACH Community Action Job Summary: The IACH is hiring a Research Assistant/Associate to help manage collaborative research and evaluation projects with clinical and community health partners. The position will collaborate with other members of the research and evaluation team across several IACH projects. Job Description: Key Responsibilities: Learn about emerging research methodologies and best practices in public health, social sciences, and community-based participatory research (CBPR). Provide indirect supervision and mentorship to junior research staff such as research assistants and student research assistants to ensure completion of study deliverables. Schedule and help facilitate study meetings with internal and external partners, including ensuring development of meeting agendas and meeting minutes. Represent IACH in academic, professional, and community settings, fostering partnerships that advance research and programmatic goals. Help monitor progress towards study timelines and deliverables using technology solutions (e.g. Asana). Conduct systematic reviews and literature reviews to ensure the use of evidence-based practices and appropriate study methodology. Assist with thedevelopment of study materials including study instruments (surveys, focus group/interview guides), data collection protocols, IRB applications, etc. Provide training to internal research staff and external partners on data collection procedures and intervention implementation as needed. Collect quantitative and qualitative study data and oversee adherence to data collection protocols. Develop and maintain study databases (RED Cap and Qualtrics), including the development of study codebook and coding (skip logic, piping, etc.).  Use descriptive and inferential statistics to conduct data analyses. Assist with the development and submission of peer-reviewed manuscripts. Assist with the development and submission of grants to support IACH research and evaluation activities. Help compile information for and develop grant reports and community presentations with internal and external partners. Follow policies and procedures related to the submission of grant related expenses in alignment with funding agreements. Employees may be requested to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically present in the description.  Relationships : Continuous contact with members of own work unit to plan, coordinate, problem solve, exchange information, and make suggestions related to mutual and institutional goals. Frequent interaction with IACH partners to exchange information, engage in creative problem solving and conduct collaborative evaluations. While this position does not have direct reports, they will be responsible for indirect supervision of junior research staff including delegating tasks and reviewing work. Responsibility for Final Decisions : Responsible for own work.  Reviews work to ensure compliance with policies, procedures, and deadlines. Spends time planning, scheduling, and assigning priorities of several ongoing assignments. Reviews work to ensure compliance with policies, procedures, and deadlines. Tasks involve interpretation of data and/or procedures to address problems or situations that are not clearly defined.  Work involves non-routine/diverse work not defined by procedures. Education/Skill Requirements :       Requires a degree in a related field such as public health, social work or other health-related field with one year of previous research experience. Master’s degree preferred. Previous experience in applied health research or program evaluation. Bachelor’s degree requires four years of experience. Master’s degree requires two years of experience. A PhD may be substituted for previous post graduate research experience. Knowledge of community-engaged or Community Based Participatory Research (CBPR) methods. Demonstrated and progressive experience in leading research and evaluation projects in community or clinical settings. Previous experience with quantitative data analysis such a logistic regression, linear regression, and structural equation modeling required Previous qualitative research experience preferred. Experience with data analytic platforms such as SAS, Stata, SPSS, NVivo, etc. Previous experience in developing academic publications including journal manuscripts, conference abstracts, etc. preferred   Previous experience working with community partners in the implementation of or evaluation of community health interventions preferred      Effective oral and written communication skills Strong organizational skills and ability to balance deliverables across multiple projects and timelines Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Custodian_R003404": {
      "title": "Custodian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Custodian Job Family: Service Workers Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Hourly Department: Clark Murphy Complex Job Summary: To maintain the cleanliness and overall appearance of Furman’s buildings, except the Dining and Residence halls, including the interior, windows and the entrances and walkways within 20 feet of the building. Job Description: Responsibilities: 1 Maintains the overall appearance by: Waxing, buffing, dusting, sweeping and mopping or vacuuming floors and stairs. Cleaning and disinfecting showers, sinks, drains, and grout in restroom and ensuring supplies are provided. Cleaning glass doors, mirrors and windows, furniture, vents, baseboard in hallways and kitchens, partitions and displays. Dusting. Cleaning/dusting venetian blinds. Cleaning and polishing brass. Cleaning water fountains. Cleaning out sand urns. Emptying and washing trash cans. Emptying recycling bins. Cleaning exterior area (20 feet) by blowing/sweeping walkways, picking up trash, and washing windows. Cleaning kitchen areas as needed. Pressure washing walks, buildings as assigned. Loading trash into dumpsters. Cleaning light fixtures/chandeliers, vents. 2. Assists in \"set-up\" and \"break down\" for meetings and special events as needed. 3. Refers work order to Facilities Services when repairs cannot be complete in-house. 4. Coordinates work schedules with building coordinator and/or occupants of areas to be cleaned. 5. Inspects building for unsafe conditions and either corrects the problem or notifies supervisor of the need to correct the problem. 6. Maintains appearance of custodial storage rooms. 7. Maintains custodial equipment assigned by following operating, storage and cleaning procedures and notifying supervisor of problems as they occur. 8. Performs special tasks as assigned by the Facilities Services manager or supervisor in support of the goals and objectives of the University. 9. Moves furniture as requested needed to perform cleaning tasks. 10. Follows safety procedures. 11. Completes performance review process with supervisor/employees to understand job performance expectations and how they fit into the department and university goals. 12. Communicates openly and recognizes contributions of co-workers and supervisor. 13. Pursues professional development to keep pace with demands for services and information; including maintaining up to date information about the department and university. Relationships: Daily works with members of own work unit, immediate supervisor, and members of other departments to exchange factual information and explanation of rules, regulations, practices or functions. Discussion of non-controversial matters. Responsibility for Final Decisions: Responsible for own work. May assist or instruct other doing similar work; including student workers. Tasks are well-defined and repetitive, following prescribed steps, methods and/or procedures. Work involves limited choice with regard to the selection of appropriate tools, methods or materials. Errors are readily detected in the normal course of work by standard check or crosscheck. Reports to: Custodial Supervisor Completed work is referred to supervisor for review. Education/Skill Requirements: High School graduate or GED with 1 – 2 years experience in related field. Ability to read and understand written instructions and product labels. Ability to operate basic cleaning equipment. Ability to complete work independently. Work Conditions: Work is performed 90% inside under usual office/gym conditions and 10% outside under varied weather conditions. Continuous exposure to cleaning chemicals, dust, dirt, and requires following safety precautions. Frequently requires lifting of up to 30 lbs. unassisted and up to 50 lbs. with assistance. Frequently requires reaching, handling, stooping, kneeling, crouching, crawling, pushing, and pulling. Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
