    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Financial-Aid-Counselor_R003417": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Financial Aid Counselor Job Family: Professionals Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Salary Department: Financial Aid-2 Job Summary: The Financial Aid Counselor, under the direction of the Associate Director of Financial Aid, will provide financial aid counseling to prospective students, their parents, and to our current students and their parents. This includes providing timely and accurate information about the processes for applying for financial aid at Furman, the timing of our decisions, the resources available to finance the costs of education, the role of borrowing and working, and how the “financial fit” of an institution pertains to choosing to apply to and enrolling at Furman. This position works within Furman’s Enrollment Management Department, a holistic “applicant-to-graduate” unit that has the strategic and operational responsibility for meeting the institution’s enrollment goals. Job Description: Position Responsibilities: Research and interpret student financial aid program guidelines including but not limited to those in the Federal Register, student aid handbooks, and training manuals to ensure compliance with federal and state regulations and program requirements. Meet with students and parents concerning the financial aid process and deadlines for applying for aid according to established procedures, and assist in completing the required forms. Provide friendly and knowledgeable assistance to applicants throughout the process. Advise students and parents of requirements and processes. Give clear and concise directions about awarding to students and how the process of allocating funds to their accounts occurs. Assist with the processing of loan files and corrections on a timely basis with accuracy within PowerFAIDS, ELM, and Common Origination Disbursement (COD). Award loans to student accounts and adjust awards as needed. Continue to work as a team member to back up for PowerFAIDS and EDConnect processing when others are not available. Counsel students concerning satisfactory academic progress, withdrawals, and enrollment plans when related to all South Carolina state aid. Counsel students regarding their grade point average and credit requirements and the effects of any dropped, failed, or withdrawals on their South Carolina state aid eligibility. Reconcile state aid programs monthly. Ensure the funds received from the state match the disbursed funds in PowerFAIDS and Workday. Operate all financial aid software programs. Perform data entry and processing. Review, analyze, and package student awards according to institutional, state, and federal requirements as it pertains to all financial aid funds. Understand and articulate the student aid awarding process. Advise current and prospective students, parents, and other interested persons about financial aid programs and requirements. Conduct financial aid workshops and training sessions. Be available to travel and work a flexible schedule as needed to assist in institutional effectiveness. Analyze and evaluate the results of need analysis to accurately determine maximum financial need and aid eligibility for students. Stay abreast of conditions that affect financial aid eligibility. Participate in national, regional, and state conferences on student financial aid programs, practices, and related technology and communicate any information that may be needed by other Furman University departments. Perform other duties as required: The intent of this job classification description is to provide a representative summary of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions in this classification and shall not be construed as a declaration of the specific duties and responsibilities of any position. Employees may be requested to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically present in this description, and some of those tasks may require night and/or weekend work.  Relationships: Continuous contact with members of own work unit and immediate supervisor to exchange factual information and explanation of rules, regulations, practices and/or functions.  Frequent contact with students, parents, other departments, student workers, and general public to exchange factual information and explanation of rules, regulations, practices and/or functions. Responsibility for Final Decisions :  Responsible for own work, and must assume responsibility for errors and corrective action(s).   May assist or instruct others doing similar work, including student workers.  Some tasks are well defined and repetitive, following prescribed steps, methods and/or procedures, though much work is upon own initiative and creativity.  Errors are readily detected in the normal course of work by standard check or crosscheck.  Work is performed independently on routine work; non-routine issues or questions are referred to and/or handled in consultation with supervisor or Executive Director of Financial Aid as well as the associate director(s) of financial aid. Reports to/ Supervisor: Reports to the Associate Director of Financial Aid Required Education, Skills, and Training: Bachelor’s degree and experience in higher education administration. Willingness and availability to work outside of normal business hours when workload requires such. Ability to communicate effectively with others, including the appropriate use of grammar and mechanics. Ability to work as a team with inter-office and intra-office personnel to accomplish office goals and responsibilities. Must have excellent organizational skills and attention to detail.  Experience in common office software, particularly Microsoft Word, Excel, and PowerPoint.  Experience with social networking a plus.  Work Conditions : Work is performed under usual office conditions. Education Requirements: Bachelors (Required) Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Financial-Aid-Counselor_R003409": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Financial Aid Counselor Job Family: Professionals Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Salary Department: Financial Aid-2 Job Summary: The Financial Aid Counselor, under the direction of the Associate Director of Financial Aid, will provide financial aid counseling to prospective students, their parents, and to our current students and their parents. This includes providing timely and accurate information about the processes for applying for financial aid at Furman, the timing of our decisions, the resources available to finance the costs of education, the role of borrowing and working, and how the “financial fit” of an institution pertains to choosing to apply to and enrolling at Furman. This position works within Furman’s Enrollment Management Department, a holistic “applicant-to-graduate” unit that has the strategic and operational responsibility for meeting the institution’s enrollment goals. Job Description: Position Responsibilities: Serve as a member of the financial aid counseling staff. This involves appointments with students and parents (both prospective and currently enrolled), reviewing and/or revising award packages, speaking to those calling in for information, assisting Enrollment Services “front line” personnel with financial aid-related questions, etc.  Administer the federal loans, federal grant, and alternative loan programs including all reporting to the Department of Education. Administration of these programs includes the monthly reconciliation of funds awarded to students, reported to the federal entities, disbursed to the students’ accounts, and posted to Furman’s general ledger.  Serve as the Federal Work Study liaison with Enrollment Services, requiring a thorough knowledge of Federal Work Study regulations and performing monthly reconciliations of Federal Work Study expenditures.  Assume creative outreach responsibilities to currently enrolled and prospective students and their parents, when appropriate. These efforts may include social media, campus announcements, etc. All work is done via standards set by the University.  Assist with Title IV requirements for verification of the data submitted by parents and students on the Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA).  Read FAFSA information and analyze income tax returns, particularly those of business owners. Make appropriate adjustments to reported income based on tax information.  Perform appropriate review of Special Financial Circumstance Forms submitted by students.  Assist with over-award monitoring throughout the year.  Present financial aid workshops on campus and in high schools in the Greenville-Spartanburg area, some that occur at nights and on weekends.   Participate in state and regional financial aid professional associations as approved by the Senior Director of Financial Aid.  Perform other duties as required: The intent of this job classification description is to provide a representative summary of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions in this classification and shall not be construed as a declaration of the specific duties and responsibilities of any position. Employees may be requested to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically present in this description, and some of those tasks may require night and/or weekend work.  Relationships:   Continuous contact with members of own work unit and immediate supervisor to exchange factual information and explanation of rules, regulations, practices and/or functions.  Frequent contact with students, parents, other departments, student workers, and general public to exchange factual information and explanation of rules, regulations, practices and/or functions.  Responsibility for Final Decisions :   Responsible for own work, and must assume responsibility for errors and corrective action(s).   May assist or instruct others doing similar work, including student workers.  Some tasks are well defined and repetitive, following prescribed steps, methods and/or procedures, though much work is upon own initiative and creativity.  Errors are readily detected in the normal course of work by standard check or crosscheck.  Work is performed independently on routine work; non-routine issues or questions are referred to and/or handled in consultation with supervisor or Executive Director of Financial Aid as well as the associate director(s) of financial aid.  Reports to/ Supervisor: Reports to the Associate Director of Financial Aid Required Education, Skills, and Training: Bachelor’s degree and experience in higher education administration. Willingness and availability to work outside of normal business hours when workload requires such. Ability to communicate effectively with others, including the appropriate use of grammar and mechanics. Ability to work as a team with inter-office and intra-office personnel to accomplish office goals and responsibilities. Must have excellent organizational skills and attention to detail.  Experience in common office software, particularly Microsoft Word, Excel, and PowerPoint.  Experience with social networking a plus.  Work Conditions : Work is performed under usual office conditions. Education Requirements: Bachelors (Required) Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Network-Administrator_R003414": {
      "title": "Network Administrator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Network Administrator Job Family: Professionals Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Salary Department: Systems and Networks Job Summary: Under the direction of the Director of Systems and Networks (or immediate supervisor), the Network Administrator responds to and diagnoses network-based functions of the campus network. This position requires intermediate level expertise in supporting and troubleshooting Ethernet over copper and optical fiber cabling. It requires a solid working foundation in networking and systems concepts and implementations. Excellent troubleshooting skills are required. This position is intended to provide Level II intermediate support between the Help Desk and Systems and Networks. As an important member of the Systems and Networks team, the Network Administrator provides troubleshooting and support of wired and wireless campus network connectivity, network access control, and other functions in support of the campus network. Knowledge and use of network monitoring systems and network troubleshooting tools is required. Strong foundation in OSI layers 1–3 and other networking standards is essential. The Network Administrator is also instrumental in the planning, configuring, and administering of wired and wireless network systems as determined by the Director of Systems and Networks (or immediate supervisor). Additionally, the Network Administrator performs primary and backup roles as assigned and is expected to maintain a high degree of technical knowledge and ability to learn new concepts quickly. Strategic network design, enterprise architecture, and major infrastructure decisions remain under the direction of the Director of Systems and Networks. Job Description: Responsibilities: Network Infrastructure Administration & Operations • Administers wired and wireless network infrastructure within established operational parameters, escalating significant or system-wide changes to departmental leadership. • Performs support, maintenance, and troubleshooting of building-based network infrastructure within established operational standards and change management guidelines. • Performs routine indoor and outdoor network cabling adds, moves, and changes independently; coordinates with departmental leadership for significant cabling infrastructure changes or new installations. • Coordinate network equipment installations, adds, moves, changes, and decommissioning activities. • Perform routine maintenance, upgrades, firmware patching, and lifecycle replacement activities. • Maintain network documentation, rack diagrams, topology diagrams, standards, and standard operating procedures. Incident Resolution, Monitoring & Level II Support • Provides field support and serves as the initial point of escalation for campus network connectivity and network user support beyond the Help Desk. • Monitor network health, performance, availability, and security utilizing network management and monitoring tools. • Analyze, diagnose, and resolve network incidents and service disruptions in a timely manner. • Support indoor and outdoor Ethernet and fiber-optic physical plant infrastructure, including testing, patching, and troubleshooting physical layer connectivity. Network Security & Systems Collaboration • Provides support when needed to the Information Security team, assisting with network access control systems and campus network security initiatives. • Assist with enforcement of network security policies, VLAN segmentation, 802.1X authentication, and secure remote access configurations. • Work with third-party vendors to provide support for residence hall networking and IPTV systems for general use. • Collaborate with ITS staff, vendors, and contractors on network-related projects and services. • Provide backup support for enterprise Unified Communications systems. Team Collaboration & Professional Development • Serves as an active member of the Systems and Networks team by: • Participating in the Systems and Networks on-call rotation. • Providing backup support to other team members. • Keeping abreast of information necessary to provide support for network systems and infrastructure. • Maintains a high degree of accountability, trustworthiness, confidentiality, and personal integrity in the management and maintenance of all network systems and access levels provisioned and administered. • Maintains current skills necessary to assume primary and secondary responsibilities. • Applies current technology to meet requirements and improve network operations. • Completes performance review process with supervisor to understand job performance expectations and how they fit into the department and university goals. • Pursues professional development to keep pace with demands for services and information, including maintaining up-to-date knowledge of networking technologies, standards, and best practices. • Performs other duties as assigned.   Relationships Daily contact with members of own work unit, immediate supervisor, and members of other departments to plan, coordinate, problem solve, present information, and make suggestions related to mutual goals. Frequent contact with student employees, other departmental staff, liaison faculty and staff to plan, coordinate, problem solve, present information, and share information. Frequent communication with vendor/contractor teams to facilitate project direction and understanding. Occasional contact with faculty, staff, and students to achieve successful outcomes related to job duties. Occasional communication and/or consultation with vendor/contractors, telecommunications carriers, and low voltage/fiber cabling contractors to facilitate project direction and understanding.   Responsibility for Final Decisions Tasks involve multiple technical procedures with interrelated processes and dependencies. Work involves choosing the appropriate option or procedure to follow and applying established rules and procedures. Work is performed independently on routine network administration and operational tasks; non-routine changes, high-risk reconfigurations, and system-wide modifications are subject to change management review and Director of Systems and Networks approval prior to execution. Errors may be detected within the department but may affect campus network availability; established change management and rollback procedures are in place to minimize impact and facilitate correction. Routine work is frequently performed independently; non-routine issues or questions are referred to the supervisor. Emergency work may call for quick independent decisions requiring close communication with the immediate supervisor.   Education/Skill Requirements Education Associate degree or equivalent with a minimum of 2 years of related experience required. Bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Network Engineering, or a related technical field from an accredited four-year college or university preferred. Related Information Technology experience may substitute for the required education on a year-for-year basis. Experience • Minimum of 2–3 years of hands-on experience administering, configuring, and supporting enterprise-grade wired and wireless network infrastructure. • Tier II problem resolution in support of wired and wireless networks. • Demonstrated experience with network monitoring tools, packet analysis/troubleshooting, and structured copper/fiber cabling infrastructure. Preferred Qualifications • Industry certifications such as Cisco Certified Network Associate (CCNA), CompTIA Network+, CWNA, or equivalent vendor certifications preferred but not required. • Familiarity with enterprise switching, wireless controllers, and network access control (NAC) preferred but not required. • Experience supporting enterprise VoIP/SIP telecommunications systems preferred but not required. Skills • Strong understanding of TCP/IP networking, subnetting, VLANs, trunking, spanning-tree protocols, and routing fundamentals. • Working knowledge of enterprise wireless standards (802.11ax/ac), RF fundamentals, SSID management, and wireless troubleshooting techniques. • Experience with network monitoring and management tools (SNMP, NetFlow/sFlow, syslog, Wireshark). • Practical understanding of structured cabling standards (Cat6/6A, single-mode and multi-mode fiber optics, patch panels, and optical transceivers). • Strong interpersonal, communication, and collaboration skills. • Ability to perform well both independently and as a member of a team. • Ability to manage multiple priorities.   Work Conditions Work is performed in a combination of office and field settings. A significant portion of time is spent in campus wiring rooms, IDF closets, and outdoor environments performing hands-on network infrastructure maintenance, cabling, and troubleshooting. Work may involve exposure to varied weather conditions and physical demands including crouching, crawling, pushing, pulling, climbing, balancing, lifting, carrying, stooping, kneeling, reaching, and handling. Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Athletics-Compliance-Director_R003416": {
      "title": "Athletics Compliance Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Athletics Compliance Director Job Family: Professionals Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Salary Department: Athletic Director-1 Job Summary: This is a professional position responsible for assisting with the administration of the department of athletics. Responsible for assisting the Associate Athletics Director for Compliance with the administration of a comprehensive compliance program that includes rules education, monitoring, reporting and general support of the compliance efforts of all student-athletes, staff, and external constituents. Works under moderate supervision, with limited latitude for the use of initiative and independent judgment. Reports to the Associate Athletics Director for Compliance and Academic Services Job Description: Oversee all aspects of either NCAA Bylaws 12, 13, 14, & 17 or NCAA Bylaws 13, 15, 16 & 17 Serve as liaison for institutional name/image/likeness (NIL) program and collaborate with campus and external partners to plan and deliver education related to NIL. Monitor and track all NIL activities to ensure compliance and reporting requirements are met Oversee Transfer Portal and Roster management Complete and assist with NCAA annual reports Assist in playing and practice season declaration and monitor student-athlete time management Recruit and Oversee Compliance Intern Manage and monitor athletics coaches and staff members as it relates to their specific NCAA role classification to ensure full compliance with their responsibilities. Educate institutional and athletics staff members, faculty, student-athletes, community members and boosters on NCAA rules and procedures. Assist with developing and implementing athletics compliance policies and procedures. Research NCAA rules and provide coaches and staff with interpretations as directed/assigned by the Associate AD for Compliance.  Work with the Southern Conference office and the NCAA to obtain official rules interpretations as needed. Investigate secondary and major NCAA violations Works to uphold the integrity of the NCAA, Conference and Athletic Department's vision and core values. Attend regional and national conferences. Other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications: A Bachelor’s degree in a related field and three years of related experience. Knowledge and understanding of NCAA rules and regulations as an administrator. Preferred qualifications include a Master's Degree, experience interpreting NCAA rules and educating a broad constituent base. Skills: Strong organizational, interpersonal, verbal and written communications skills. Basic Problem solving skills Ability to compile data and prepare, write and review reports. Excellent computer skills with a working knowledge of MS Office and the ability to learn compliance and institutional software. Experience utilizing Canva software Education Requirements: Bachelors (Required) Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Groundskeeper_R003413": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Groundskeeper Job Family: Service Workers Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Hourly Department: Campus Grounds Job Summary: To maintain the grass, shrubbery, flowers, trees, and general appearance of the campus property. Job Description: Responsibilities : 1. Maintains the grass on campus property by: Mowing, using tractor mowers, push mowers, 7 gang mower. Operating weedeaters, edgers, clippers. Aerating, sodding, and seeding by using tractors, spreaders, tillers, etc. Watering grass using irrigation system, water truck, sprinklers and hoses. Picking up litter, limbs, dead animals, etc using shovel, stick, broom, hands. Spraying chemicals to kill weeds and treat diseases and to fertilize using backpack sprayer or tractor sprayer. Vacuuming or raking leaves using vacuum unit, rake or backpack leaf blower. Clearing lots (as needed) using bushhog. 2. Maintains shrubbery, plants, trees and flower beds by: Pruning flowers (roses), shrubs and trees using hand or gasoline powered shears, chainsaws and ladders or bucket truck as necessary. Planting flowers, shrubs and trees by using shovels, spades, backhoe, etc. Mulching and weeding shrubbery and flower beds. Spraying chemicals to kill weeds, diseases, or to fertilize plants using backpack or hand sprayer. Removing shrubs, flowers, tree stumps using backhoe, shovel and stump grinder. Trims limbs on campus trees using chainsaws, pole clippers and bucket truck. 3. Maintains cleanliness of walkways, parking lots and lake by: Blowing leaves and grass from the sidewalk/parking lot/road using backpack blower, push blowers, vacuum truck, tractor with blower attachment. Cleaning out storm drains by using shovels to remove leaves, sand and dirt. Maintaining the lake by skimming the water to remove debris, dead animals, etc. and by using the motor boat to trap and assist injured ducks and swans. Shoveling snow/ice from walkways as necessary. 4. Maintains grounds equipment by: Washing mowers, tractors, etc. Sharpening blades. Checking oil, fuel and filters for cleanliness or replacement. 5. Maintains and repairs grounds irrigation systems by: Repairing broken sprinkler heads, valves, pipes, etc. Clearing blockages from valves and pipes. Inspecting and servicing automatic sprinkler control devices, electric and hydraulic systems and pumping systems. 6. Completes additional duties assigned by supervisor. 7. Completes performance review process with supervisor/employees to understand job      performance expectations and how they fit into the department and university goals. 8. Communicates openly and recognizes contributions of co-workers and supervisor. 9. Pursues professional development to keep pace with demands for services and   information; including maintaining up to date information about the department and university. Relationships : Continuous contact with members of their own work unit and immediate supervisor to exchange factual information and explanation of rules, regulations, practices or functions. Discussion of non-controversial matters. Frequent contact with students, general public to exchange factual information and explanation of rules, regulations, practices or functions.  Discussion of non-controversial matters. Responsibility for Final Decisions : Tasks are well-defined and repetitive, following prescribed steps, methods and/or procedures. Work involves limited choice with regard to the selection of appropriate tools, methods or materials. Errors are readily detected in the normal course of work by standard check or crosscheck.  Reports To :   Groundskeeper Foreman Completed work is referred to supervisor for review. Education/Skill Requirements : High School graduate with 1 – 2 years experience in related field. Ability to read and understand instructions for the use of machinery, chemicals and seed/fertilizer. Valid S.C. Drivers License. Experience in the operation of grounds tools/equipment. Horticultural degree or equivalent experience preferred. Work Conditions: Work is performed primarily outside under variable weather conditions with daily exposure to noise, dust, heat, cold, dampness, dirt, chemicals and fumes. Work requires: Frequent lifting grounds equipment into and out of pick up trucks. (ie: backpack blowers, sprayers, chainsaws, weedeaters, etc. up to 50 lbs.) Frequent reaching, bending, stooping when pruning shrubs, flowers and hedges. Frequent pushing mowers, blowers, and edgers. Frequent driving/operating mowers/tractors, etc. using handles, foot pedals and hand levers. Frequent operating weedeaters by holding them above the ground and moving them back and forth while walking. Frequent recognizing disease, problems to report to supervisor. Frequent lifting bags (up to 50 lbs) of seed, fertilizer, etc. Frequent using hand tools such as spades, clippers, and shovels. Groundskeepers may be subject to call-back if there is an emergency, i.e.: tree down, irrigation line broken, injured duck or swan. Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    },
    "https://furman.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Furman_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Groundskeeper_R003411": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nWelcome to Furman University's Career Site! IMPORTANT :​ Load all documents in the My Experience area under Resume/CV . This may include your resume/C.V., cover letter, unofficial transcript, teaching philosophy, etc. Please wait for all documents to finish uploading before clicking Next. If the process times out you will lose your progress, so please ensure you have all necessary documents available before starting. The drop-down lists only display so many options: please type into a drop-down list to search for your option. For example: in Field of Study, you may need to enter your field name. If you have no field, type \"None.\" If you have previously applied, make sure your information is current as you can transfer it to another application. Prior to submitting your application, verify all information for accuracy and ensure you have uploaded all appropriate documents.  Once submitted, you will not be able to edit your application other than to change your contact information. If you have any questions or need additional assistance, please contact the Office of Human Resources via email at recruitment@furman.edu  or by calling 864-294-3103. Job Title: Groundskeeper Job Family: Service Workers Full-Time/Part-Time: Full time Pay Type: Hourly Department: Athletic Grounds Job Summary: The Groundskeeper position is responsible for maintaining the appearance, safety, and functionality of campus and/or athletic grounds. This position performs routine and seasonal landscaping, grounds maintenance, and irrigation work to ensure an attractive and welcoming campus environment for students, staff, and visitors. Job Description: Essential Duties and Responsibilities Grounds and Turf Maintenance •Mow, edge, and trim campus lawns using tractor mowers, push mowers, gang mowers, and hand tools. •Operate weed eaters, edgers, clippers, and backpack blowers. •Perform aeration, sodding, seeding, fertilizing, and irrigation of turf areas. •Collect and dispose of litter, fallen limbs, leaves, and debris. •Apply fertilizers, herbicides, and pesticides in accordance with safety standards and regulations. •Clear overgrown areas using bush hogs or other appropriate equipment. Landscape, Tree, and Plant Care •Prune and trim shrubs, flowers, and trees using hand tools, power equipment, chainsaws, ladders, or bucket trucks. •Plant, transplant, and remove flowers, shrubs, and trees using hand tools or heavy equipment. •Mulch and weed flower beds and landscaped areas. •Remove tree stumps and unwanted vegetation using stump grinders, backhoes, or other equipment. •Identify plant disease, pest issues, or safety concerns and report them to supervision. Walkways, Parking Lots, and Water Features •Maintain cleanliness of sidewalks, parking lots, roads, and common areas by blowing or vacuuming debris. •Clean storm drains and drainage systems to prevent blockages. •Maintain campus lake and surrounding areas by removing debris and assisting with injured wildlife using approved methods and equipment. •Remove snow and ice from walkways and entrances as needed. Equipment and Irrigation Maintenance •Clean, inspect, and perform basic maintenance on grounds equipment and vehicles. •Sharpen blades and check oil, fuel, and filters. •Assist Irrigation Technician with repairing irrigation systems, including sprinkler heads, valves, pipes, and controllers. Professional Responsibilities •Follow all safety procedures and proper use of personal protective equipment (PPE). •Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, students, and the public. •Participate in performance reviews and contribute to department and university goals. •Pursue ongoing professional development related to grounds maintenance and landscaping. •Perform other duties as assigned by the supervisor. Relationships and Communication •Regular interaction with immediate supervisor and work unit to exchange information and coordinate tasks. •Frequent contact with students, staff, and the general public to provide information and assistance related to campus grounds. Decision-Making Authority •Performs assigned tasks following established procedures and guidelines. •Selects appropriate tools and equipment for routine work. •Completed work is reviewed by the supervisor. Education and Experience Requirements •High school diploma or equivalent. •One to two (1–2) years of experience in groundskeeping, landscaping, or a related field. •Valid South Carolina driver’s license. •Ability to read and understand instructions for equipment operation, chemicals, and materials. •Experience operating grounds maintenance tools and equipment. •Horticulture degree or equivalent experience preferred. Work Conditions and Physical Requirements •Work is performed primarily outdoors in varying weather conditions. •Regular exposure to noise, dust, heat, cold, chemicals, and fumes. •Frequent lifting and carrying of equipment and materials up to 70 pounds. •Frequent bending, stooping, reaching, pushing, pulling, and walking. •Regular operation of mowers, tractors, blowers, weed eaters, and hand tools. On-Call Responsibilities Groundskeepers may be subject to call-back during emergencies, including downed trees, snow and/or ice removal, or special events on campus. Reports To: Senior Groundskeeper, Assistant Superintendent of Campus Grounds/Athletic Grounds and Superintendent of Grounds, Horticulture, and Sports Turf Education Requirements: Certification Requirements: Job Posting End Date (if date is blank, posting is open ended):"
    }
  },
